docs/arnold/native-platform.md:13:| Reconcile | Resume and expired-lease takeover run `arnold.pipeline.native.reconcile` before mutation; dirty unknown worktrees, in-progress git operations, and unproven idempotent effects block. | Unit tests may feed deterministic fake `ReconcileDecision` values instead of probing a real repo. | Do not continue a resumed or takeover run until reconcile returns `execute`, `continue_owned`, or `skip`. |
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:17:| DB durability | Durable store rows, operation records, lease records, and audit refs are the production authority across process death. | File stores and temporary JSON state are acceptable for deterministic conformance. | Do not treat local memory as durable production state. |
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:22:| Rollout | New platform behavior rolls through local conformance, installed-package conformance, canary, then operator-enabled production. | Developers may run focused suites against a checkout. | Do not promote canary-only or local-only paths as production-covered. |
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:35:Production evidence must work from the installed package, not only from the
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:52:| Platform must not become a hidden owner of product routing, loop exits, model routing, execute/review decisions, or task satisfaction. | Delivered by platform. | Platform E2E and chain/PR conformance assert substrate metadata never owns those product decisions. |
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:53:| Durable suspension, approval, resume, and cancellation across process death. | Delivered by platform for local conformance; production rollout requires backend/operator setup. | `tests/arnold/conformance/test_platform_e2e.py`; operator prerequisite in `docs/arnold/operations.md`. |
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:56:| File backend as production durability proof. | Intentionally deferred. | File backend is local-only conformance; downstream owner: platform operations to provision Postgres before production admission. |
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:58:| Full final native-representation closeout report, YAML ledger, proof map, and manifest. | Deferred to later batch. | Owner: M6 final ledger tasks; this batch only updates docs and conformance tests. |
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:66:| DBOS output cache leaking into replay semantics. | Rejected for M6. | DBOS remains disabled by the persistence decision record until cached replay becomes an explicit policy with conformance. |
docs/arnold/native-platform.md:76:| Rollout mode | Canary first, then explicit operator enablement. | Local conformance, installed-wheel conformance, canary audit refs, cancellation proof, and signed rollout record. |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-07-generated-docs-skills-parity.txt:18:`docs/reference/arnold-projections.md` and runtime docs must describe event-journal projections, artifact roots, and resume cursors. M3 defines the new event journal as single source of truth; M4 maps old `.megaplan` state. But M5's requirement to "update docs" doesn't include a conformance test proving that documented state shapes match actual `arnold.execution` event schemas. Projection docs could describe old `state.json` fields long after the runtime has switched to event sourcing.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-07-generated-docs-skills-parity.txt:39:> The package authoring contract (`docs/arnold/package-authoring-contract.md`) must specify that `build_pipeline()`-bearing packages provide a `regenerate_artifacts()` or equivalent hook, and that generated skills/docs/dispositions must pass a semantic conformance gate (not just clean-diff).
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-07-generated-docs-skills-parity.txt:47:> 13. A semantic conformance test suite exercises generated pipeline examples from docs, skills, and scaffolds through compile → dry-run → fake-run → resume, failing if any documented example diverges from runtime behavior.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-07-generated-docs-skills-parity.txt:49:### M6: Strengthen artifact conformance gate
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-07-generated-docs-skills-parity.txt:52:> Enforce generated-artifact semantic conformance: every shipped generated skill, composed rule, projection doc, scaffold template, package disposition entry, and pipeline registry row must pass (a) provenance-freshness check, (b) import-surface scan against surviving M6 public API, (c) compile-and-fake-run of any embedded pipeline example, and (d) diff against regeneration-from-current-generators yielding zero drift.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-07-generated-docs-skills-parity.txt:62:| **Semantic compile gate** | Every code block in skills/docs compiles against current installed public surface | M5 done criteria; M6 conformance |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-07-generated-docs-skills-parity.txt:65:| **Import surface scan on generated content** | Generated skills/docs/dispositions contain zero references to M6-deleted modules, classes, or entrypoints | M6 conformance (must use dynamic + string-aware scan, not just AST) |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-07-generated-docs-skills-parity.txt:68:The plan's core weakness is treating generated artifacts as "disposable outputs" (M5 Locked Decisions) while simultaneously expecting them to teach correct behavior. Disposability and correctness are in tension: if they're disposable, the plan needs less rigor; if they're teaching surfaces, they need the same semantic conformance as code. The plan currently splits the difference without resolving it.
docs/arnold/workflow-authoring.md:106:The audit skeleton is the evidence layer for conformance verification, not a
docs/arnold/native-composition-metadata-plan.md:14:  native IR so that composition, trace, replay, and conformance tooling can
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-09-test-evidence-parity.txt:32:M6 says conformance allowlists must "burn down to zero legacy exceptions or re-chartered with owner, expiry, and non-legacy rationale." Re-chartering is an explicit alternative to deletion. Combined with dynamic-import detection that must catch `importlib`, `__getattr__`, and `__all__` — complex to fully audit — this gate is vulnerable to "we audited it, remaining entries are re-chartered."
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave7/w7-09-test-evidence-parity.txt:45:| **m6-clean-break-purge-conformance.md** | Remove "re-charter" as an allowlist disposition. Allowlist rows must be deleted or proven to have zero legacy import surface via static + dynamic scan with no exception. If re-chartering is kept, require an independent review gate. |
