docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-02-cli-entrypoint-deletion-attack.txt:7:**1. The `arnold/cli/__init__.py` lazy-import trap.** The ONLY production dispatch path — `arnold/__main__.py` → `arnold.cli.cli_entry()` → `main()` — does `from arnold.pipelines.megaplan.cli.arnold import main as _arnold_main` *inside a function*. A static AST scan sees zero imports of deleted modules at module load time. If M6's conformance test stops at `import arnold` without actually invoking `python -m arnold <subcommand>` from an installed wheel, deletion falsely passes — the import succeeds but the CLI crashes on first use.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-02-cli-entrypoint-deletion-attack.txt:17:**6. 30+ test files import old CLI paths directly.** Tests in `test_feedback.py`, `test_epic_cli.py`, `test_pipeline_runnable_e2e.py`, `conftest.py`, `test_override_strict_notes.py`, `test_chain_worktree_base_ref.py` and many others all import `arnold.pipelines.megaplan.cli` at module level. If these tests are updated to import from `arnold_pipelines.megaplan.cli` (new location) but the old `arnold.pipelines.megaplan.cli` modules are deleted, deletion "passes" only if every such test was either migrated or deleted — a combinatorially large conformance check that's easy to under-audit.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-02-cli-entrypoint-deletion-attack.txt:38:| 5 | **Dynamic-import trap: monkeypatch `importlib.import_module` and `__import__` during the full conformance suite to detect ANY import of a module matching deleted path prefixes.** | M6 Done Criterion #4 says "no production code … dynamically imports deleted surfaces" but defines no enforcement mechanism. |
