docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-01-public-import-deletion-attack.txt:9:**2. Installed-wheel staleness.** The current `pyproject.toml` builds `packages = ["arnold"]` into the wheel before deletion. If the M6 conformance suite tests against an editable install (where files are truly deleted) but never rebuilds the wheel and re-installs from it, the wheel that ships to users still contains every old module. A subprocess import test from a clean venv with the stale wheel passes locally but the published artifact is dirty.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-01-public-import-deletion-attack.txt:11:**3. `arnold/pipeline/__init__.py` keeps `arnold.runtime` alive transitively.** The file currently does `from arnold.runtime.envelope import RuntimeEnvelope` and re-exports it in `__all__`. If M6 deletes `arnold/runtime/` but neglects to strip those re-export lines from `arnold/pipeline/__init__.py`, the import fails at the re-exporter — but if M6 wraps them in `try/except ImportError` to make the conformance import scan pass, old consumers that `from arnold.pipeline import RuntimeEnvelope` see it silently swallowed. Either way, the re-export chokepoint must be removed cleanly.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-01-public-import-deletion-attack.txt:17:**6. `arnold/pipeline/__init__.py` re-exports of `PipelineBuilder`, `Stage`, `Edge`, `run_pipeline`, etc.** M6 Done Criterion #3 says `from arnold.pipeline import Stage` must fail. But `Stage` is defined in `arnold/pipeline/types.py` and re-exported. If M6 removes the re-export from `__init__.py` but doesn't delete `types.py`, `from arnold.pipeline.types import Stage` still works — and every old consumer that uses the dotted path survives. The conformance gate must test both `__init__` re-exports and full dotted imports of every public symbol.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-01-public-import-deletion-attack.txt:25:**10. Module-level side effects suppressed but not removed.** `arnold/pipelines/megaplan/__init__.py` calls `_register_megaplan_content_types()` and `_install_model_adapter_once()` at import time. If the deletion check catches `ImportError`, the gate is green, but these initialization steps silently don't run for the new path — behavior diverges without any test noticing because the conformance suite never exercises the old-init-then-fail path.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/wave9/w9-01-public-import-deletion-attack.txt:47:**Plan edits to `m6-clean-break-purge-conformance.md`:**
