docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-10-amendments-postmerge.txt:12:- **Post-merge safety net**: `chain.yaml`'s `post_merge_conformance` and M6 Done Criterion 31 recheck the integrated result so merge order cannot resurrect stale contracts or deleted surfaces.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-10-amendments-postmerge.txt:20:The plan doesn't model an explicit "if amendment occurred, re-run upstream gates before proceeding" mechanism. It relies on (a) sequential `depends_on` preventing downstream start until upstream completion, and (b) `post_merge_conformance` as the catch-all. If M4 amends M1 contracts during its branch, M2 and M3 are already "done" — there's no forced re-validation of M2/M3 against the amended M1 until post-merge. This works but is a softer guarantee than an explicit re-gate.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-10-amendments-postmerge.txt:25:2. **M6 scope, add**: "If deletion/conformance work discovers a contract gap that was masked by deleted surfaces, update M1/M2/M3 contract tests and `workflow-manifest-amendments.md`."
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-10-amendments-postmerge.txt:26:3. **chain.yaml `post_merge_conformance`**: add a sentence — "If any downstream milestone amended M1/M2/M3 contracts, re-run the full upstream milestone gate suite (not only the amended tests) before declaring merge-result conformance green."
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-03-neutral-product-boundary.txt:13:| Neutral runtime never imports product modules directly | Every milestone: M1 Done Criteria #5 (import-boundary conformance); M3 Done Criteria #5 (execution imports no product modules); M6 gates against dynamic `importlib`, `__getattr__`, and `TYPE_CHECKING` leaks. |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-03-neutral-product-boundary.txt:14:| No resolving manifest strings at runtime | M3: "manifest refs are identifiers, not runtime import instructions"; "must not call `importlib.import_module` on any manifest field." M6 reinforces with full import-tracing conformance. |
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-03-neutral-product-boundary.txt:18:1. **`arnold.agent` omission.** M1 treats `arnold.agent`, `arnold.control`, and `arnold.conformance` as neutral surfaces if they survive, subject to the same import-boundary rules. M4 allows product to depend on `arnold.agent`. The answer should list `arnold.agent` alongside the four neutral packages — or explain it's implicitly included under `arnold.kernel`/`arnold.execution`.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-08-shipped-generated-surfaces.txt:12:- "Independent review" appears only at M6 for conformance allowlists. "No old-native teaching surface" is implicit in zero-public-surface (no docs, no generated skills), but a whitelisted pipeline could technically pass public-surface checks yet still contain copy-pasteable native decorators or builder patterns that a developer would read.
docs/arnold/workflow-manifest-runtime-review/subagent-results/load-bearing-questions/lbq-06-state-resume.txt:16:| "No surviving runtime/operator path may depend on old `state.json` authority after M6" | M6 criterion 28: filesystem-read trace proves no surviving path reads old `.megaplan` or `state.json` authority outside sunset-expired migration/projection modules. M6 scope: "Instrument filesystem reads during conformance" to enforce this. |
